Z||000000||Neal||06-08-2001||10:02 PM||||I ripped apart a compleate fiero enging that i recently bought as a COMPLEATE unit.  EVERYTHING including wireing harness was there.<BR>The strange thing you may ask the heads were the non H.O. heads, 1.6 and 1.3 inch valves.<P>I was counting on these heads for my 3.4 buildup, can i have my machineist install the larger valves on these heads? or should i continue my search elsewhere?||||reg||11
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||06-09-2001||12:01 PM||||Im not sure if the 2.8 heads can be made into the HO heads because Im not sure if there was anything else different between the two besides the valves. One would think that GM would have been too cheap to change anything else other than the valves on these parts. <P>It wasn't uncommon for some unsuspecting Fiero owner who thought he was getting the right 2.8 long block to get the the 2.8 block used in a multitude of different GM products. Only Fiero 2.8s at the time had the larger valve heads. I would say consult your machinist to see if the heads can accept the larger valves and then port and polish to your heart's content!||||reg||4
Z||000002||Neal||06-09-2001||08:29 PM||||Talked to my machinist this morning,he works sat till noon, and he is trying to track down another H.O. head for me because the heads are common enough that its not woth the work.<P>Also i didnt buy a longblock i bought the entire engine fully assembled with ALL of the accessorys, so it must have been a foactory screw up||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||06-10-2001||12:06 AM||||According to the Chevy Power book, the only difference in the heads is the valve size. Casting is the same. Ports are the same. FWIW.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<P>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"||||reg||1
